(()=>{
const fx=TRACKS.find(t=>t.id==='javafx'),css=TRACKS.find(t=>t.id==='css'),sw=TRACKS.find(t=>t.id==='swift');
fx.units.push({name:'Dialogs, Windows & Lists',lessons:[
{name:'Alerts & Dialogs',sheet:['Alert.AlertType picks the icon: INFORMATION, WARNING, ERROR, CONFIRMATION.','showAndWait() returns Optional — empty means the user dismissed the dialog.','TextInputDialog gives back Optional<String>, not a raw String.','Create and show dialogs on the JavaFX Application Thread.'],tip:'Alerts and dialogs are short modal windows. Alert covers the common cases (info, warning, error, yes/no). TextInputDialog asks for a line of text. showAndWait() blocks until the dialog closes and returns an Optional so you can tell Cancel from OK.',ex:[
M('Which class shows a standard warning, error, or yes/no window?',['Alert','Label','Tooltip','MenuBar'],0,'javafx.scene.control.Alert. Pick a type with Alert.AlertType.'),
F('Show a confirmation and wait for the user.','Optional<ButtonType> r = alert.___();',['showAndWait','show','open','await'],0,'showAndWait() blocks the caller and returns Optional<ButtonType>. show() returns immediately.'),
M('The user closes a confirmation alert with the window X, without pressing a button. showAndWait() returns…',['ButtonType.CANCEL','An empty Optional','null','ButtonType.CLOSE always'],1,'Dismissing the dialog produces Optional.empty(). Test with isPresent() or ifPresent().'),
F('Ask the user to type a name.','TextInputDialog d = new TextInputDialog("Ada");\nOptional<String> name = d.___();',['showAndWait','getText','readLine','prompt'],0,'TextInputDialog.showAndWait() returns Optional<String>. The constructor argument is the default text.'),
M('You call label.setText on a property that is currently bound. What happens?',['The text changes','RuntimeException: a bound value cannot be set','The binding is removed','Nothing, silently'],1,'Unbind first, or write to the source of the binding instead. This is the same rule as textProperty().bind().'),
B('The dialog is built on a background thread. Tap the line that is unsafe.',['Task<Void> task = new Task<>() {','    protected Void call() {','        Alert a = new Alert(Alert.AlertType.INFORMATION);','        return null;','    }','};'],2,'Alert (like any live scene-graph node) must be created on the JavaFX Application Thread. Build it before the task, or inside Platform.runLater.'),
A('Arrange a confirmation that prints OK only when the user agrees.',['Alert a = new Alert(Alert.AlertType.CONFIRMATION, "Delete?");','Optional<ButtonType> r = a.showAndWait();','if (r.isPresent() && r.get() == ButtonType.OK) {','    System.out.println("OK");','}'],'Show the alert, then check the Optional before using the button.'),
M('ButtonType.OK == r.get() is a safe comparison because…',['All strings compare with ==','ButtonType constants are singletons, like enum values','== compares the button label','It is never safe'],1,'The predefined ButtonType constants are shared instances, so == matches the one the user pressed.')]},
{name:'Windows & Modality',sheet:['A Stage is a window. initOwner ties it to a parent window.','initModality(APPLICATION_MODAL) blocks this app\'s other windows.','Call initOwner and initModality before show().','show() returns immediately; showAndWait() waits.'],tip:'A second Stage is just another window. initOwner attaches it to a parent so it stays in front and closes with that parent. Modality decides whether the user can click the rest of the app while the window is open. Both must be set before the stage is shown.',ex:[
M('APPLICATION_MODAL means the new window…',['Freezes the operating system','Blocks input to the other windows of this application','Only dims the owner window\'s title bar','Runs on a background thread'],1,'OpenJFX Modality.APPLICATION_MODAL blocks events to any other window in the same application.'),
F('Make dialog stay on top of the window that opened it.','dialog.initOwner(___);',['primaryStage','dialog','Scene','Modality.NONE'],0,'initOwner takes the parent Window. The owned window is hidden when its owner is hidden.'),
M('When must you call initModality?',['Any time, even after the window is visible','Before the stage is shown','Only inside stop()','After showAndWait returns'],1,'The Stage docs require modality to be initialized before the window becomes visible.'),
F('Open a window without waiting for it to close.','stage.___();',['show','showAndWait','open','launch'],0,'show() displays the stage and returns immediately. showAndWait() does not return until it closes.'),
M('A WINDOW_MODAL stage blocks…',['Every window in the application','Only the owner window and the windows it owns','Only windows of other applications','Nothing'],1,'WINDOW_MODAL is narrower than APPLICATION_MODAL: it blocks the owner chain, not the whole app.'),
M('Which is the default modality of a new Stage?',['APPLICATION_MODAL','WINDOW_MODAL','NONE','TOOL'],2,'Modality.NONE: the user can keep using other windows. Dialogs opt into modality.'),
B('The owner is set too late. Tap the line that breaks the rule.',['Stage d = new Stage();','d.initModality(Modality.APPLICATION_MODAL);','d.show();','d.initOwner(primaryStage);'],3,'initOwner, like initModality, has to happen before show(). After show() the window is already on screen.'),
A('Arrange an owned modal window.',['Stage d = new Stage();','d.initOwner(primaryStage);','d.initModality(Modality.APPLICATION_MODAL);','d.setScene(new Scene(new Label("Saved"), 200, 80));','d.showAndWait();'],'Configure owner and modality, attach a scene, then wait.')]},
{name:'Lists & Selection',sheet:['ListView displays an ObservableList and refreshes when that list changes.','getSelectionModel().getSelectedItem() is null when nothing is selected.','SelectionMode.MULTIPLE allows more than one row.','Listen to selectedItemProperty instead of polling.'],tip:'ListView is the standard scrollable list. Give it an ObservableList (FXCollections.observableArrayList) and the rows update when you add or remove items. The selection model tells you what the user picked — always handle the "nothing selected" case.',ex:[
F('Read the single selected row.','String pick = list.getSelectionModel().___();',['getSelectedItem','getValue','getText','selected'],0,'getSelectedItem() returns the item, or null if the selection is empty.'),
M('Why pass an ObservableList instead of an ordinary ArrayList?',['ObservableList uses less memory','The ListView repaints when the list changes','ArrayList cannot hold Strings','It enables multiple selection'],1,'Observable collections fire change events. The control listens and updates itself.'),
F('Allow selecting more than one row.','list.getSelectionModel().setSelectionMode(SelectionMode.___);',['MULTIPLE','MANY','ALL','EXTENDED'],0,'SelectionMode.MULTIPLE. The default is SINGLE.'),
M('getSelectedItem() when the user has not clicked anything returns…',['""','0','null','Optional.empty()'],1,'There is no selection, so the result is null. Check it before calling methods on it.'),
F('Build a list the ListView can observe.','ObservableList<String> items = FXCollections.___("A", "B");',['observableArrayList','observable','asList','copyOf'],0,'FXCollections.observableArrayList wraps the items in a list that fires updates.'),
M('selectedItemProperty().addListener(...) is useful because…',['It sorts the list','It runs your code whenever the selection changes','It selects every row','It replaces setOnAction on buttons'],1,'The property is observable, so you react to changes instead of asking on a timer.'),
B('Tap the line that throws if nothing is selected.',['String pick = list.getSelectionModel().getSelectedItem();','int n = pick.length();','list.getItems().add("new");'],1,'pick is null when the selection is empty, so pick.length() throws NullPointerException. Test pick != null first.'),
A('Arrange a listener that prints the newly selected name.',['list.getSelectionModel().selectedItemProperty()','    .addListener((obs, prev, cur) -> {','        System.out.println(cur);','    });'],'The change listener receives the old value and the new value.')]}]});
css.units.push({name:'Forms, Position & Access',lessons:[
{name:'Forms & Validation',sheet:[':valid and :invalid reflect the browser\'s built-in constraint checks.','A <label for="id"> increases the tap target of its input.',':focus-visible draws a ring for keyboard focus without flashing it on every click.','Put the error text in the page, not only in a change of border color.'],tip:'Browsers already check required, type="email", min, and pattern. :invalid and :valid let you style that state. Pair every input with a real <label>, and don\'t rely on color alone to say "this is wrong" — add text a screen reader can announce.',ex:[
M('Which selector matches an email input whose value fails type="email"?',[':invalid',':error',':bad',':empty'],0,'The :invalid pseudo-class matches form controls that fail constraint validation.'),
M(':focus-visible is preferred over :focus for a focus ring because…',['It is shorter to type','The browser shows it for keyboard focus and skips many mouse clicks','It works only in forms','It changes the value'],1,'Users who tab need a visible ring. People clicking with a pointer usually don\'t.'),
F('Style the input only while its value fails validation.','input___ { border-color: #b91c1c; }',[':invalid',':required',':focus','::invalid'],0,'Pseudo-classes use one colon. ::invalid is not a thing.'),
M('Clicking a <label for="email"> should…',['Submit the form','Move focus to the input with id="email"','Hide the input','Validate the page'],1,'That also makes the label\'s text part of the control\'s hit area — important on a phone.'),
F('A sibling hint directly after the input.','input:focus ___ .hint { display: block; }',['+','>','~','::'],0,'+ is the adjacent-sibling combinator: the .hint that comes immediately after the input.'),
M('Coloring a border red, with no text, fails which idea?',['The cascade','Don\'t use color as the only way to convey an error','Specificity','The box model'],1,'WCAG 1.4.1: people who cannot see the color still need the message. Add words.'),
M('autocomplete="email" on an input…',['Disables validation','Hints the browser (and password managers) which value to offer','Requires JavaScript','Hides the label'],1,'It is an HTML attribute. It does not replace a <label>.'),
A('Arrange a labeled required email field.',['<label for="email">Email</label>','<input id="email" type="email" required>','<p class="hint">Use a real address.</p>'],'The label\'s for matches the input id. required turns on :invalid while it is empty.')]},
{name:'Positioning',sheet:['static is the default and ignores top/left.','relative offsets the box but keeps its original space.','absolute is taken out of flow and anchored to the nearest positioned ancestor.','sticky acts like relative until a scroll threshold, then sticks.','fixed is anchored to the viewport, unless an ancestor has transform, filter, or will-change.'],tip:'position chooses the anchor. relative nudges a box without removing it. absolute and fixed take the box out of flow. sticky is the one you want for a bar that scrolls with the page and then locks. Always give a positioned ancestor (position: relative) if an absolute child should line up with a card rather than the page.',ex:[
M('top: 8px on a position: static element…',['Moves it down 8px','Does nothing — static ignores top, right, bottom, and left','Sticks it to the viewport','Centers it'],1,'The offsets only apply to relative, absolute, sticky, and fixed.'),
M('position: relative; top: 8px does what to the layout around the box?',['The gap it left behind collapses','It shifts visually, but the original space is still reserved','It is removed from flow','Later siblings move up by 8px'],1,'Relative offsets are visual. Neighbors stay where they were.'),
F('Anchor an absolute child to its card, not the page.','.card { position: ___; }',['relative','static','float','inline'],0,'A non-static ancestor becomes the containing block for absolute descendants.'),
M('position: sticky; top: 0 is closest to…',['Always fixed to the viewport','Relative while it can, then fixed once it would scroll away','Absolute at the bottom of the parent','The same as static'],1,'MDN: sticky is treated as relative until it crosses a threshold, then as fixed inside its containing block.'),
M('An ancestor with transform: translateZ(0) changes position: fixed so that it…',['Stops working entirely','Is positioned relative to that ancestor instead of the viewport','Ignores z-index','Becomes sticky'],1,'transform, filter, and will-change on an ancestor create a new containing block for fixed descendants.'),
F('Keep a toolbar visible at the top of the screen while the page scrolls.','header { position: ___; top: 0; }',['fixed','static','inherit','initial'],0,'fixed stays relative to the viewport. sticky is the alternative when it should stop at the end of a section.'),
M('Two absolute siblings overlap. Which one paints on top by default?',['The one that comes first in the HTML','The one that comes later in the HTML','Whichever has the larger font','Random'],1,'Without z-index, later in the tree paints later (on top), for positioned boxes in the same stacking context.'),
A('Arrange CSS that pins a badge to the top-right of a card.',['.card { position: relative; }','.badge {','    position: absolute;','    top: 8px; right: 8px;','}'],'The card is the anchor. The badge leaves normal flow and sits in the corner.')]},
{name:'Accessible CSS',sheet:['prefers-reduced-motion: reduce means cut animation, not the content.','prefers-color-scheme lets you follow the system light or dark setting.','display: none removes an element from the accessibility tree.','A "visually hidden" clip pattern keeps text available to screen readers.','Never delete a focus outline unless you replace it with something obvious.'],tip:'CSS can respect the user\'s OS settings and still hide things the wrong way. display: none and visibility: hidden drop content from the accessibility tree. If you only want sighted users to skip a label, clip it off screen instead. And a focus outline is not decoration — it is how keyboard users see where they are.',ex:[
F('Turn animations off for people who asked the OS for less motion.','@media (___: reduce) { .node { animation: none; } }',['prefers-reduced-motion','prefers-color-scheme','motion','reduced'],0,'The media feature is prefers-reduced-motion, and the value you match is reduce.'),
M('display: none on a "skip to content" link means…',['Screen readers still read it','It is removed from the accessibility tree as well as the screen','It is invisible but still focusable','It only hides on phones'],1,'If it must stay available to assistive tech, don\'t use display: none.'),
M('prefers-color-scheme: dark matches when…',['The page has a dark class','The user\'s system theme is dark','It is after 8pm','The monitor is OLED'],1,'You can follow the system, and still offer your own toggle on top.'),
M('Which rule removes an element from the accessibility tree?',['display: none','color: transparent','opacity: 0','font-size: 0'],0,'display: none (and visibility: hidden) remove the element from the accessibility tree. A clip rectangle does not.'),
M('outline: none on :focus with no replacement…',['Is required for a modern look','Hides the keyboard focus indicator','Only affects Firefox','Improves contrast'],1,'Replace it with :focus-visible { outline: 3px solid ... } rather than deleting the ring.'),
M('Which media query follows the system light/dark setting?',['@media (color-scheme: dark)','@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)','@media (theme: dark)','@media (dark)'],1,'prefers-color-scheme is the standard media feature. color-scheme is a property, not the query name.'),
M('A 44×44 CSS pixel tap target comes from…',['A CSS default you cannot change','WCAG 2.5.5 / platform guidance, which you meet with padding and min-height','Only native apps','The font-size property alone'],1,'Padding counts toward the target. Tiny icon buttons need a bigger hit area.'),
A('Arrange a reduced-motion override.',['@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {','.path .nodewrap { animation: none; }','}'],'Match the media feature, then remove the motion inside the block.')]}]});
sw.units.push({name:'Strings, Errors & SwiftUI',lessons:[
{name:'Strings & Characters',sheet:['let makes a constant. var can be reassigned.','Strings are value types. "A" + "B" builds a new String.','count is the number of Character values, not the number of bytes.','\\(expression) drops a value into a string.','uppercased() returns a new string; it does not change the original.'],tip:'Swift strings are Unicode text, not raw bytes. count walks extended grapheme clusters (what a reader calls a character). Interpolation ("Hi \\(name)") builds a new String. Methods like uppercased() return a new value because String is a value type — the original stays put unless you assign the result back.',ex:[
O('let name = "Ada"\nprint("Hi \\(name)")',['Hi Ada','Hi \\(name)','Hi name','Ada'],0,'\\(name) is string interpolation. The Swift book builds a new String with the value dropped in, so print writes Hi Ada.'),
O('let s = "ab"\nprint(s.count, s.uppercased())',['2 AB','2 ab','1 AB','AB'],0,'count is 2 Characters. uppercased() returns a new String "AB". print separates items with a space.'),
O('let a = [10, 20, 30]\nprint(a[0] + a.count)',['13','10','30','103'],0,'Arrays are zero-based, so a[0] is 10. count is 3. 10 + 3 = 13.'),
O('var bag = ["pen"]\nbag.append("pad")\nprint(bag.joined(separator: "-"))',['pen-pad','penpad','["pen", "pad"]','pad-pen'],0,'append mutates the var array. joined(separator:) concatenates the Strings with "-" between them.'),
M('Why does "café".count sometimes surprise people who counted bytes?',['count is always 1','count is Character values (extended grapheme clusters), not UTF-8 bytes','count includes the quotes','Swift strings have no count'],1,'The Swift book: count is the number of Characters. One visible character can be more than one Unicode scalar.'),
F('Build a greeting without concatenation.','let msg = "Hello, ___user)"',['\\(','${','+','%'],0,'Write "Hello, \\(user)". The backslash and parentheses mark interpolation.'),
B('Tap the line that will not compile.',['let title = "ByteRyte"','title = "Other"','print(title)'],1,'let is a constant. Assigning again is a compile error. Use var if the name must change.'),
M('s.uppercased() when s is a let String…',['Changes s','Returns a new String and leaves s as it was','Crashes','Only works on var'],1,'String methods that "change" text return a new value. Assign it if you want to keep it.')]},
{name:'Throwing Functions',sheet:['throws on the function, throw inside it, try at the call.','do/catch handles the error. The error value is in scope as error.','try? turns a failure into nil instead of throwing.','try! crashes if the call throws — avoid it unless failure is impossible.','?? supplies a default when try? produced nil.'],tip:'Swift errors are values, not exception class hierarchies. Mark a function throws, throw an error value, and call it with try. do/catch is the full handle. try? is the "I only care whether it worked" form: you get an Optional. try! is a force unwrap of that and will crash — treat it like ! on nil.',ex:[
O('enum E: Error { case bad }\nfunc f() throws -> String { throw E.bad }\nprint((try? f()) ?? "fallback")',['fallback','bad','nil','Crash'],0,'try? converts the thrown error into nil. ?? then substitutes "fallback". The Swift book documents both in Error Handling and Optionals.'),
F('Mark a function that can fail with a thrown error.','func load() ___ -> String',['throws','throw','try','catch'],0,'throws goes on the declaration, before the return arrow. try is only at the call site.'),
M('Inside catch, the thrown value is available as…',['e','exception','error','err'],1,'A catch clause with no pattern binds the error to the local name error.'),
F('Call load() and turn failure into nil.','let text = ___ load()',['try?','try!','catch','throws'],0,'try? returns an Optional: the value, or nil if the function threw.'),
M('try! load() when load throws…',['Returns nil','Crashes at runtime','Becomes an empty String','Is a compile error'],1,'try! force-unwraps the result of a throwing call. A throw becomes a crash. Prefer try or try?.'),
B('Tap the line that will not compile.',['enum E: Error { case bad }','func load() throws -> String { throw E.bad }','let s = load()'],2,'A throwing function must be called with try, try?, or try!. A bare call does not compile.'),
O('let n: Int? = nil\nprint(n ?? 4)',['4','nil','0','Optional(4)'],0,'?? unwraps nil to the default. print then writes the Int 4, not Optional(4).'),
A('Arrange a do/catch around a throwing call.',['do {','    let s = try load()','    print(s)','} catch {','    print(error)','}'],'try stays inside do. catch handles whatever load throws.')]},
{name:'SwiftUI Lists',sheet:['A View is a struct with a body.','@State stores view-owned state. Changing it re-renders the body.','ForEach needs a stable identity: id: \\.self for simple values, or Identifiable.','List is the scrolling container. ForEach is the loop.','Button("Save") { } runs the trailing closure when tapped.'],tip:'SwiftUI describes the UI as a function of state. @State is state the view owns. When it changes, body is called again and the framework updates the screen. List plus ForEach is the usual way to show a collection. ForEach must know how to tell rows apart, so you pass an id.',ex:[
O('let names = ["Bo", "Ada"]\nprint(names.sorted().joined(separator: ","))',['Ada,Bo','Bo,Ada','Ada Bo','["Ada", "Bo"]'],0,'sorted() returns a new array in ascending order: Ada then Bo. joined(separator: ",") puts a comma between them and does not add spaces.'),
F('Loop over strings, using each string as its own identity.','ForEach(names, id: \\.___) { name in Text(name) }',['self','id','name','0'],0,'\\.self uses the value itself as the identity. Fine for unique strings. Use a real id if values can repeat.'),
M('@State private var count = 0 inside a View means…',['A global variable','State this view owns; writing to it refreshes body','A constant','A binding to a parent'],1,'@State is the source of truth. Pass $count (a Binding) if a child needs to write it.'),
F('A button that runs a closure when tapped.','Button("Next") ___ print("go") }',['{','=>','->','do'],0,'SwiftUI buttons take a trailing closure as the action: Button("Next") { print("go") }.'),
M('List versus ForEach:',['They are the same','List is the scrolling container; ForEach produces the rows','ForEach scrolls, List does not','List only works with arrays of Int'],1,'You usually write List { ForEach(...) { ... } }. ForEach can also sit in a VStack.'),
M('Why does ForEach want an id?',['To color the rows','So SwiftUI can tell which row moved, was inserted, or was deleted','It is optional syntax sugar','To sort the data'],1,'Identity is how the framework matches old rows to new ones when state changes.'),
B('Tap the line that will not compile.',['struct Row: View {','    body: some View {','        Text("Hi")','    }','}'],1,'body has to be a property: var body: some View. Without var this is not a valid declaration, so the View does not compile.'),
A('Arrange a tiny SwiftUI screen with a list of two names.',['struct Names: View {','    let names = ["Ada", "Bo"]','    var body: some View {','        List(names, id: \\.self) { Text($0) }','    }','}'],'Conform to View, store the data, and return a List from body.')]}]});
})();
(()=>{
const j=TRACKS.find(t=>t.id==='java');
j.units.push({name:'Unit 6 · Deeper Java',lessons:[
{name:'Abstract vs Interface',sheet:['A class extends one class. It can implement many interfaces.','Interface methods are public. Interface fields are public static final.','An abstract class can hold fields, constructors, and a mix of abstract and concrete methods.','If two interfaces give the same default method, the class must override it and can call A.super.m().','static methods on an interface belong to the interface. Implementers do not inherit them.'],tip:'Use an interface for a capability other types can mix in: Readable, Comparable, Runnable. Use an abstract class when subclasses share real state and a partial implementation. A template method (a final method that calls an abstract one) lives naturally on an abstract class. You only get one superclass, so spend it when you need shared fields.',ex:[
M('Subclasses must share a mutable field and a method that uses it. What fits?',['An interface','An abstract class','A record','An enum'],1,'Interfaces cannot declare instance fields. An abstract class can hold the field and the shared method.'),
O('interface A { default String id() { return "A"; } }\ninterface B { default String id() { return "B"; } }\nclass C implements A, B {\n    public String id() { return A.super.id() + B.super.id(); }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new C().id());',['A','B','AB','Compile error'],2,'Two defaults with the same signature conflict. The class overrides id() and picks each one with Interface.super.'),
O('abstract class Job {\n    final String label() { return kind() + " job"; }\n    abstract String kind();\n}\nclass Mail extends Job { String kind() { return "mail"; } }\n// in main:\nJob j = new Mail();\nSystem.out.println(j.label());',['mail','job','mail job','Compile error'],2,'label() is the template: it is final and shared. kind() is the piece each subclass fills in. The object is a Mail, so kind() returns mail.'),
O('interface Lim { int MAX = 3; }\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(Lim.MAX);',['3','null','0','Compile error'],0,'A field in an interface is public, static, and final. You read it on the interface name. There is no instance.'),
O('interface U { static String hi() { return "hi"; } }\nclass V implements U {}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(U.hi());',['hi','null','Compile error','V'],0,'A static interface method is called on the interface: U.hi(). It is not inherited, so V.hi() would not compile.'),
X('Dog says it implements Pet, but the compiler rejects name(). Which line replaces the method?','interface Pet {\n    String name();\n}\nclass Dog implements Pet {\n    String name() { return "Rex"; }\n}',['    public String name() { return "Rex"; }','    String name() { return "Rex"; }','    private String name() { return "Rex"; }','    static String name() { return "Rex"; }'],0,'Interface methods are public. The override must be public too. private is more restrictive, and static does not implement the instance method.'),
F('Pick interface A\'s default when two defaults clash.','return A.___.id();',['super','this','parent','base'],0,'The form is A.super.id(). super alone would mean the class superclass.'),
M('An abstract class implements an interface but leaves one method abstract. That…',['Is a compile error','Is legal: a concrete subclass has to finish the method','Instantiates the interface','Makes the method static'],1,'Abstract types may pass an unfinished contract down. Only the concrete class must implement every abstract method.')]},
{name:'Generic Methods',sheet:['A generic method declares its own type variable: <T> T first(List<T> xs).','Bounds: <T extends Comparable<T>> means T can be compared with itself.','? extends Number is a producer. You can read a Number out. You cannot add elements.','? super Integer is a consumer. You can add an Integer. This is PECS: producer-extends, consumer-super.','At runtime the type arguments are erased. List<String> and List<Integer> share one class.'],tip:'List<String> stops the wrong element at compile time. Generic methods go further: one method works for many types, and the compiler infers T from the arguments. Bounds limit what T can do. Wildcards show up when a method only reads (? extends) or only writes (? super). Erasure is why instanceof List<String> is illegal and why a raw list can pollute a typed one.',ex:[
O('static <T> T first(List<T> xs) { return xs.get(0); }\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(first(List.of("Ada", "Bo")));',['Ada','Bo','[Ada, Bo]','T'],0,'The compiler infers T = String from the list. first returns the element at index 0.'),
O('static <T extends Comparable<T>> T bigger(T a, T b) {\n    return a.compareTo(b) >= 0 ? a : b;\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(bigger(3, 8));',['3','8','11','Compile error'],1,'Integer implements Comparable<Integer>, so the bound is satisfied. compareTo says 8 is larger.'),
O('static double sum(List<? extends Number> xs) {\n    double s = 0;\n    for (Number n : xs) s += n.doubleValue();\n    return s;\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(sum(List.of(1, 2, 3)));',['6','6.0','123','Compile error'],1,'? extends Number accepts List<Integer>. The total is a double, so println writes 6.0.'),
O('List<String> a = new ArrayList<>();\nList<Integer> b = new ArrayList<>();\nSystem.out.println(a.getClass() == b.getClass());',['true','false','Compile error','null'],0,'Type arguments are erased. Both lists are ArrayList at runtime, so the classes are equal.'),
O('List<? extends Number> nums = List.of(1, 2.5);\nSystem.out.println(nums.get(0).getClass().getSimpleName());',['Number','Integer','Double','Object'],1,'The list holds Number, but the value at index 0 is an Integer. 2.5 would be a Double.'),
X('push should accept List<Integer>, List<Number>, or List<Object> and add 1. Which signature replaces the first line?','static void push(List<Integer> dest) {\n    dest.add(1);\n}',['static void push(List<? super Integer> dest) {','static void push(List<? extends Integer> dest) {','static void push(List<?> dest) {','static void push(List<Number> dest) {'],0,'A consumer uses ? super Integer, so add(1) is legal and List<Number> or List<Object> can be passed. ? extends and ? do not allow add. List<Number> does not accept a List<Integer>.'),
M('List<? extends Number> nums. Which call is legal besides add(null)?',['nums.add(1)','nums.add(1.5)','Reading a Number with nums.get(0)','nums.add(new Object())'],2,'extends is a producer. The compiler does not know the real element type, so it refuses to add values. get returns a Number.'),
M('Why is `o instanceof List<String>` rejected?',['List is not a class','Generics are erased, so the runtime cannot tell List<String> from List<Integer>','instanceof only works for primitives','String is final'],1,'Use instanceof List or instanceof List<?> . The <String> part does not exist at runtime.')]},
{name:'Checked & Unchecked',sheet:['Checked exceptions extend Exception and are not RuntimeException. The compiler makes you catch them or declare throws.','Unchecked exceptions extend RuntimeException. NullPointerException and IllegalArgumentException are unchecked.','try-with-resources closes AutoCloseable resources in reverse order, before catch.','If the body and close() both throw, the body exception wins and the close exception is added with addSuppressed.','throw raises an object. throws only appears on a method signature. Multi-catch types cannot be subclasses of each other.'],tip:'Checked means "callers must have a plan": files, network, anything IOException. Unchecked means "this is a bug or a bad argument": the compiler stays quiet. try-with-resources is the safe way to close things, because close still runs when the body throws, and it runs before your catch. Order matters: close the last resource first.',ex:[
O('class R implements AutoCloseable {\n    final String n;\n    R(String n) { this.n = n; System.out.print("open" + n); }\n    public void close() { System.out.print("close" + n); }\n}\n// in main:\ntry (R a = new R("A"); R b = new R("B")) {\n    System.out.print("body");\n}',['openAopenBbodycloseAcloseB','openAopenBbodycloseBcloseA','bodyopenAopenB','closeBcloseAbody'],1,'Resources are created left to right and closed in reverse: B then A. close runs after the body.'),
O('try {\n    try {\n        throw new IllegalStateException("boom");\n    } finally {\n        System.out.print("F");\n    }\n} catch (RuntimeException e) {\n    System.out.print(e.getMessage());\n}',['boomF','Fboom','boom','F'],1,'finally runs before the exception leaves the inner try. The outer catch then prints the message.'),
O('try {\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n} catch (IllegalArgumentException | IllegalStateException e) {\n    System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());\n}',['IllegalArgumentException','IllegalStateException','Exception','RuntimeException'],0,'Multi-catch matches either type. The object that was thrown is IllegalArgumentException, so that is the class name printed.'),
O('class Bad implements AutoCloseable {\n    public void close() { throw new IllegalStateException("close"); }\n}\n// in main:\ntry (Bad b = new Bad()) {\n    throw new IllegalStateException("body");\n} catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n    System.out.println(e.getMessage() + " " + e.getSuppressed()[0].getMessage());\n}',['close body','body close','close','body'],1,'The body exception is the one caught. The exception from close() is suppressed on it, so you can still see both.'),
O('System.out.println(new RuntimeException("x") instanceof Exception);',['true','false','Compile error','x'],0,'RuntimeException extends Exception. Unchecked exceptions are still exceptions. They are just not checked by the compiler.'),
X('fail() should throw a checked IOException. Which line replaces the body?','static void fail() throws java.io.IOException {\n    throw "disk";\n}',['    throw new java.io.IOException("disk");','    throw "disk";','    throws new java.io.IOException("disk");','    throw java.io.IOException("disk");'],0,'throw takes an exception object, so you need new. throws belongs on the signature only. A constructor call without new does not compile.'),
M('Which of these is unchecked?',['IOException','FileNotFoundException','IllegalArgumentException','Exception itself, when you declare it'],2,'IllegalArgumentException extends RuntimeException. The others are checked: callers must catch them or list them in throws.'),
M('Which catch header compiles?',['catch (IOException | IllegalArgumentException e)','catch (Exception | IOException e)','catch (RuntimeException | IllegalArgumentException e)','catch (Exception | RuntimeException e)'],0,'The alternatives cannot be subclasses of each other. IOException and IllegalArgumentException are siblings. Exception already covers the others, so those headers are rejected.')]},
{name:'Stream Pipelines',sheet:['A pipeline is source, intermediate ops, then one terminal op.','flatMap turns each element into a stream and concatenates them. map keeps one output per input.','distinct and sorted are intermediate. joining, counting, and toList are terminal.','groupingBy builds a Map. counting() values are Long.','List.of is immutable. Sort a copy in an ArrayList. removeIf is the safe way to drop elements while walking a list.','findFirst returns Optional. A stream can be consumed only once.'],tip:'Streams earn their keep on multi-step transforms: flatten, keep, reshape, then collect. Nothing in the middle runs until the terminal call. groupingBy is how you turn a list into a map of buckets. Optional is how findFirst says "maybe none" without null. And a stream is a one-shot view: when you need the data twice, start a new stream from the source.',ex:[
O('var m = List.of("ada", "al", "bo").stream()\n    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(s -> s.charAt(0), Collectors.counting()));\nSystem.out.println(m.get(\'a\') + " " + m.get(\'b\'));',['2 1','1 1','2 2','a b'],0,'ada and al land in the a bucket (count 2). bo is the only b (count 1). counting() returns Long, and println prints the number.'),
O('var r = List.of(List.of(1, 2), List.of(3)).stream()\n    .flatMap(List::stream)\n    .map(n -> n * 10)\n    .toList();\nSystem.out.println(r);',['[10, 20, 30]','[[10, 20], [30]]','[10, 30]','[1, 2, 3]'],0,'flatMap joins the inner lists into 1, 2, 3. map then multiplies each by 10.'),
O('String s = List.of("b", "a", "b", "c").stream()\n    .distinct().sorted().collect(Collectors.joining());\nSystem.out.println(s);',['abc','bacb','babc','a b c'],0,'distinct keeps the first b, a, and c. sorted orders them, and joining with no delimiter glues them together.'),
O('var m = new HashMap<String, Integer>();\nm.computeIfAbsent("x", k -> 0);\nm.merge("x", 2, Integer::sum);\nSystem.out.println(m.get("x"));',['0','2','3','null'],1,'computeIfAbsent stores 0 because x is missing. merge then adds 2 with Integer::sum, so the value is 2.'),
O('List<String> xs = new ArrayList<>(List.of("a", "", "b"));\nxs.removeIf(String::isEmpty);\nSystem.out.println(xs);',['[a, b]','[a, , b]','[a]','[]'],0,'removeIf drops every element that matches, without a ConcurrentModificationException. The empty string goes away.'),
O('Optional<String> o = Stream.of("a", "bb", "ccc")\n    .filter(s -> s.length() > 5).findFirst();\nSystem.out.println(o.orElse("none"));',['a','ccc','none','null'],2,'Nothing is longer than 5 characters, so findFirst is an empty Optional. orElse supplies none.'),
O('List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(3, 1, 2));\nnums.sort(Comparator.naturalOrder());\nSystem.out.println(nums);',['[1, 2, 3]','[3, 1, 2]','[3, 2, 1]','UnsupportedOperationException'],0,'The ArrayList copy can be reordered. naturalOrder sorts ascending. List.of itself would have thrown.'),
X('sort throws because the list cannot be changed. Which line replaces the first one?','List<Integer> nums = List.of(3, 1, 2);\nnums.sort(Comparator.naturalOrder());\nSystem.out.println(nums);',['List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(3, 1, 2));','List<Integer> nums = List.of(3, 1, 2);','List<Integer> nums = List.copyOf(List.of(3, 1, 2));','List<Integer> nums = Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<>(List.of(3, 1, 2)));'],0,'List.of, List.copyOf, and unmodifiableList all refuse sort. Copy the values into a new ArrayList, then sort that.')]},
{name:'Records in Practice',sheet:['A record is a final class with private final components.','Accessors are named x(), not getX(). There is no setter.','equals, hashCode, and toString use every component.','A compact constructor can normalize the parameters before they are stored.','Records may implement interfaces and may be generic. They always extend java.lang.Record, so they cannot extend another class.'],tip:'Reach for a record when the type is data: a point, a user id, a map key. You get construction, equality, and a readable toString without writing them. Put validation in the compact constructor, which can replace a parameter (negative x becomes 0) before the field is assigned. If you need a different x, construct a new record. Do not mutate the old one.',ex:[
O('record Point(int x, int y) {\n    Point { if (x < 0) x = 0; }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new Point(-3, 4).x());',['-3','0','4','Compile error'],1,'The compact constructor runs before the fields are assigned. Assigning the parameter x changes the value that gets stored.'),
O('record Pair(String a, int b) {}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new Pair("ok", 2).hashCode() == new Pair("ok", 2).hashCode());',['true','false','Compile error','0'],0,'Two records with the same components are equal and share a hash code. That is what makes them safe HashMap keys.'),
O('record User(String name) {\n    String label() { return name.toUpperCase(); }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new User("ada").label());',['ada','Ada','ADA','User[name=ada]'],2,'A record can declare its own methods. toUpperCase returns a new String, ADA.'),
O('record Box<T>(T value) {}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new Box<>("hi").value());',['hi','T','Box[value=hi]','null'],0,'Records can take a type parameter. value() returns the component.'),
O('record Point(int x, int y) {}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(new Point(1, 2));',['Point[x=1, y=2]','(1, 2)','Point@1a2b','1,2'],0,'The generated toString lists every component. You do not write it yourself.'),
X('p is a Point record. getX() does not exist. Which line prints the x component?','record Point(int x, int y) {}\n// in main:\nPoint p = new Point(3, 4);\nSystem.out.println(p.getX());',['System.out.println(p.x());','System.out.println(p.getX());','System.out.println(p.x);','System.out.println(Point.x());'],0,'The accessor matches the component name: x(). It is not a JavaBean getter and not a public field.'),
M('A record can extend…',['Any one class you choose','Only java.lang.Record, which the compiler adds for you','Another record','An abstract class and a record'],1,'Records are final data carriers. They can implement interfaces, but the superclass slot is taken by java.lang.Record.'),
M('Where do you reject a bad component, such as a blank name?',['In a setter called later','In the compact constructor','By making the component public','You cannot validate a record'],1,'The compact constructor is the one place every construction path runs. Throw IllegalArgumentException there if the value is unusable.')]},
{name:'Enums with State',sheet:['An enum is a fixed set of constants. Each constant is the only instance of that name.','Constants can carry fields. The constructor runs once per constant.','A constant can override an abstract method declared on the enum.','valueOf and name() use the exact declared name. They are case-sensitive.','toString may be overridden. name() still returns the constant name. == is safe because constants are singletons.'],tip:'An enum is the right model for a closed set: coin denominations, traffic lights, operations. Put the data on the constant (PENNY holds 1) instead of a switch full of magic numbers. When each constant behaves differently, give the enum an abstract method and override it on ADD and MUL. valueOf("PENNY") works. valueOf("penny") throws.',ex:[
O('enum Coin {\n    PENNY(1), NICKEL(5);\n    final int cents;\n    Coin(int cents) { this.cents = cents; }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(Coin.NICKEL.cents + Coin.values().length);',['5','6','7','2'],2,'NICKEL stores 5. values() returns both constants, so the length is 2. 5 + 2 = 7.'),
O('enum Op {\n    ADD { int apply(int a, int b) { return a + b; } },\n    MUL { int apply(int a, int b) { return a * b; } };\n    abstract int apply(int a, int b);\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(Op.MUL.apply(3, 4));',['7','12','34','Compile error'],1,'MUL overrides apply. 3 * 4 = 12. The variable type can be Op and the constant supplies the body.'),
O('enum Coin {\n    PENNY(1), NICKEL(5);\n    final int cents;\n    Coin(int cents) { this.cents = cents; }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(Coin.valueOf("PENNY").name());',['PENNY','1','penny','Coin'],0,'valueOf looks up the constant by its declared name. name() returns that same text.'),
O('enum Light { RED, GREEN }\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(switch (Light.RED) {\n    case RED -> 1;\n    case GREEN -> 2;\n});',['1','2','0','RED'],0,'A switch on an enum can be exhaustive. RED selects the first arm, which yields 1.'),
O('enum Coin {\n    PENNY(1);\n    final int cents;\n    Coin(int cents) { this.cents = cents; }\n    public String toString() { return cents + "c"; }\n}\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(Coin.PENNY + " " + Coin.PENNY.name());',['PENNY PENNY','1c PENNY','1c 1c','1 PENNY'],1,'println uses toString, which was overridden to 1c. name() ignores that override and still returns PENNY.'),
X('valueOf is crashing. Which line prints S?','enum Size { S, M, L }\n// in main:\nSystem.out.println(Size.valueOf("s"));',['System.out.println(Size.valueOf("S"));','System.out.println(Size.valueOf("s"));','System.out.println(Size.valueOf("small"));','System.out.println(new Size("S"));'],0,'valueOf is case-sensitive and expects the constant name. "s" and "small" throw. new Size(...) is not allowed: the constants already exist.'),
M('How many times does an enum constructor run?',['Every time you read the constant','Once for each constant, when the enum class is initialized','Never. Enums cannot have constructors','Once per call to valueOf'],1,'PENNY(1) and NICKEL(5) are the constructor calls. They happen when the enum is initialized, not when your code mentions them later.'),
M('Comparing two enum constants with == is…',['Unsafe. Always use equals','Safe. Each constant is a singleton, and == is null-safe on the left','Only legal inside a switch','A compile error'],1,'There is only one PENNY object. == checks identity, which for enums is the same as being the same constant.')]},
{name:'Big-O Tradeoffs',sheet:['Big-O drops constants. A loop of n steps and another loop of n steps are still O(n).','O(1) stays flat. O(log n) halves the work. O(n) walks the input. O(n log n) is a good sort. O(n²) is nested work that grows with both loops.','ArrayList.add at the end is amortized O(1). Inserting at index 0 shifts every element: O(n).','HashMap.get is O(1) on average and O(n) in the worst case, when every key lands in one bucket.','Arrays.binarySearch needs a sorted array. A hit returns the index. A miss returns a negative insertion point. The result is undefined if the array is not sorted.'],tip:'Big-O is a way to compare plans, not a timer. Ask what happens when the input doubles. A second loop that also runs n times does not become quadratic: you did 2n visits, which is still linear. Nested loops that both grow with n do become quadratic. Pick the structure to match the question: a HashSet for "have I seen this?", an ArrayList for order, binary search only after the data is sorted.',ex:[
O('int n = 8, c = 0;\nfor (int i = n; i > 0; i /= 2) c++;\nSystem.out.println(c);',['8','3','4','16'],2,'i is 8, 4, 2, then 1. The next value is 0 and the loop stops. Four visits. Halving is the shape of O(log n).'),
O('int c = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)\n    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)\n        c++;\nSystem.out.println(c);',['4','6','7','16'],1,'The inner loop runs 0, then 1, then 2, then 3 times. 0+1+2+3 = 6. A triangle of visits is still O(n²).'),
O('int[] a = {1, 3, 5, 7};\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(a, 4));',['-1','2','-3','4'],2,'4 is missing. It would be inserted at index 2. binarySearch returns -(insertion point) - 1, which is -3.'),
O('int[] a = {1, 3, 5, 7};\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(a, 5));',['5','2','3','-3'],1,'The array is sorted, and 5 sits at index 2. A hit returns that index.'),
O('int ops = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) ops++;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) ops++;\nSystem.out.println(ops);',['5','10','25','32'],1,'Two loops in a row do 5 + 5 visits. That is O(n) + O(n), which simplifies to O(n), not O(n²).'),
X('This search throws on the last pass. Which line keeps it inside the array?','int found = -1;\nfor (int i = 0; i <= a.length; i++) {\n    if (a[i] == target) found = i;\n}',['for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {','for (int i = 0; i <= a.length; i++) {','for (int i = 1; i <= a.length; i++) {','for (int i = 0; i <= a.length + 1; i++) {'],0,'Legal indexes are 0 through length - 1. i <= a.length reads one past the end and throws. One forward scan is O(n).'),
M('ArrayList.add(x) at the end, versus add(0, x) at the front. Average cost for a long list?',['Both O(1)','End is amortized O(1). Front is O(n) because every later item shifts','Both O(n)','End is O(n). Front is O(1)'],1,'The end usually has spare room. The front never does: each insert moves the current elements right.'),
M('You call binarySearch on an array that is not sorted. What is true?',['It still returns the correct index, just slower','The contract does not apply. The return value is not meaningful','It throws IllegalArgumentException','It sorts the array first'],1,'Binary search trusts the order. Sort first, or scan linearly. A number that comes back from an unsorted array is not an answer you can use.')]}]});
})();
if(typeof module!=='undefined')module.exports=TRACKS;
